window.GAME_TR = window.GAME_TR || [];
window.GAME_TR.push({
  unit: 8,
  en: {
    "u.realm": "Fizzing Reaction Cave",
    "u.story": "Deep inside the Greenwood Laboratory there is a rocky cave where test tubes fizz, change colour and get hot all by themselves. The cave keeper only opens the door to those who can tell a physical change from a chemical reaction, neutralise a sour lake and identify each gas with nothing but a splint. Put on your safety glasses and step inside!",
    "s.8.4.learn": ["In a chemical reaction new products are formed from the reactants; some clues tell you that a reaction might have taken place.","Reaction 1: magnesium with acid gives off bubbles of hydrogen.","Reaction 2: baking powder with vinegar gives off carbon dioxide, which turns limewater **cloudy**.","Reaction 3: a piece of apple in hydrogen peroxide gives off oxygen, which relights a **glowing** splint.","Activity: make carbon dioxide yourself and test it with limewater.","Activity: make oxygen yourself and test it with a glowing splint.","Another clue: a reactant 'disappears' because it is used up in the reaction.","Another clue: a colour change – black copper oxide makes a blue solution of copper sulfate.","Another clue: heat is produced.","Another clue: a change in pH when you neutralise an alkali.","Another clue: a **precipitate** forms – a solid made when two solutions are mixed.","Investigation: carry out a series of experiments and use the clues to decide whether a chemical reaction has taken place."],
    "s.8.4.learnDetail.0": ["The question: how can you tell that a reaction has happened? There are some clues that might mean it has.","One of the most useful clues is whether a gas is given off. The book looks at three reactions."],
    "s.8.4.learnDetail.1": ["You saw this reaction and learnt how to test for hydrogen in Topic 8.1.","magnesium + hydrochloric acid → magnesium chloride + hydrogen."],
    "s.8.4.learnDetail.2": ["The gas given off when baking powder and vinegar react is carbon dioxide.","Test for carbon dioxide with limewater: when limewater mixes with carbon dioxide it turns cloudy.","The book's photo: limewater turns cloudy as carbon dioxide is bubbled through it."],
    "s.8.4.learnDetail.3": ["A piece of apple in hydrogen peroxide bubbles: the gas given off is oxygen (hydrogen peroxide reacts with chemicals in the apple).","Test for oxygen with a glowing splint at the mouth of the test tube.","If the gas is oxygen, the splint relights."],
    "s.8.4.learnDetail.4": ["You need: the apparatus shown. Safety: wear safety glasses.","Steps: pour limewater into a test tube until about half full; put a small amount of calcium carbonate into another test tube; add some hydrochloric acid to the calcium carbonate.","Quickly fit the rubber bung and delivery tube; make sure the delivery tube reaches into the limewater.","The book asks: describe the changes to the limewater when you tested for carbon dioxide."],
    "s.8.4.learnDetail.5": ["Steps: pour hydrogen peroxide into a test tube until about half full; add a spatula of manganese dioxide.","Place a glowing splint into the neck of the test tube.","The book asks: describe what happened to the glowing splint; how easy were the gas tests, what difficulties did you have and how did you try to overcome them?"],
    "s.8.4.learnDetail.6": ["When magnesium ribbon reacts with acid, hydrogen is produced and the ribbon 'disappears'.","The magnesium is used up in the reaction: it combines with the chlorine from the hydrochloric acid to form magnesium chloride."],
    "s.8.4.learnDetail.7": ["Gently heating black copper oxide with sulfuric acid produces a blue solution of copper sulfate.","copper oxide (black) + sulfuric acid → copper sulfate (blue) + water.","Safety: if you try this in the laboratory, do not boil the blue liquid, as harmful fumes will be given off."],
    "s.8.4.learnDetail.8": ["When potassium is placed in water, hydrogen is given off; the reaction produces so much heat that the hydrogen burns.","Safety: the potassium and water reaction should only be done by a teacher.","When you added zinc to hydrochloric acid, hydrogen was given off and the test tube felt hot."],
    "s.8.4.learnDetail.9": ["When you neutralise an alkali there is a change in pH; it is called a neutralisation reaction.","sodium hydroxide + hydrochloric acid → sodium chloride + water (acid added from a burette to a flask of sodium hydroxide).","In this reaction sodium chloride and water are produced."],
    "s.8.4.learnDetail.10": ["Mixing solutions of silver nitrate and calcium chloride causes a chemical reaction: when the two solutions (liquids) are mixed a solid forms – this solid is a precipitate.","In this example the precipitate is silver chloride: silver nitrate + calcium chloride → silver chloride + calcium nitrate.","Limewater is a solution of calcium hydroxide; it turns cloudy when carbon dioxide is bubbled in because a precipitate of calcium carbonate forms – you added a gas to a liquid and a solid formed.","calcium hydroxide + carbon dioxide → calcium carbonate + water."],
    "s.8.4.learnDetail.11": ["Safety: before trying any of these reactions you must carry out a risk assessment and think about how you will stay safe; wear safety glasses for every experiment.","A: add black copper oxide powder to about 150 cm³ of dilute sulfuric acid in a beaker and stir gently. B: add a small piece of zinc to a test tube half full of dilute hydrochloric acid; hold the tube – do you notice anything?","C: half-fill a test tube with limewater and blow gently into it through a straw. D: put a small piece of chocolate in a test tube and stand it in a beaker of hot water.","E: heat copper sulfate solution gently in an evaporating dish (on a pipe-clay triangle and tripod) until it starts to spit, turn off the heat and leave it to cool. F: add universal indicator to sodium hydroxide in a conical flask and add acid from a burette until the indicator turns green.","G: put a few spatulas of baking powder in a test tube and add vinegar. H: half-fill a test tube with silver nitrate and slowly add calcium chloride solution. The book asks for each: what do you observe, has a chemical reaction taken place, and what is your evidence?"],
    "s.8.4.extend": ["One clue alone does not prove a reaction: boiling water bubbles, a fizzy drink bubbles when opened, chocolate melts and sugar dissolves, but these are physical changes with no new substance — the chocolate sets again when cool, and evaporating sugar water gives back the sugar.","Breathed-out air contains a lot of carbon dioxide, so blowing through a straw into limewater turns it cloudy; a basin of limewater left in the yard also gets a white crust of calcium carbonate from the carbon dioxide in the air.","Potassium burns with a lilac flame. Each metal gives a flame its own colour — that is also where the colours of fireworks come from.","White silver chloride slowly darkens in light — a property once used to make black-and-white photographic film."],
    "s.8.4.figcap": ["Six clues that a chemical reaction might have taken place","The three gas tests in this topic: hydrogen, carbon dioxide and oxygen","Apparatus for testing carbon dioxide with limewater","Testing for oxygen: a glowing splint relights","Colour change: black copper oxide makes a blue copper sulfate solution","Precipitates: white silver chloride, and calcium carbonate turning limewater cloudy"],
    "s.8.3.learn": ["Scientists ask questions about neutralisation, for example: which is the best indigestion **remedy**?","A question must be precise, written in a way that can be tested by an experiment.","Activity: in a group, write down four questions about acids and alkalis that you could investigate.","Planning an investigation means designing an experiment; sometimes you must use a model instead of the real thing.","There is a lot to think about when planning; the things that change are called **variables**.","You also need to think: how you will know the acid is neutralised, what you will see, how you will do it and how you will record the results.","Activity: in a group, plan an investigation for one question, divide up the tasks and share it with the class.","Marcus and Arun investigate which indigestion powder is best at neutralising acid.","First results: powder A needed 10 spatulas, B needed 6 and C needed 24.","Repeating twice more gives three results for each powder and a mean.","The three sets of results show that you must look critically at the evidence from an investigation."],
    "s.8.3.learnDetail.0": ["A remedy is a treatment for an illness or injury.","Three example questions: how much lime should be added to an acid lake to neutralise it? Which is the best indigestion remedy? How much toothpaste is needed to neutralise the acid in your mouth?"],
    "s.8.3.learnDetail.1": ["'Which is the best indigestion remedy?' is not precise: does 'best' mean the most pleasant tasting, the cheapest, the most effective or the most cost-effective?","Instead a scientist might ask: 'Which indigestion powder neutralises the acid, using the least amount of powder?'"],
    "s.8.3.learnDetail.2": ["Work in a group of three or four; discuss and write down four questions about acids and alkalis that you could investigate.","Share your ideas with the class.","The book asks: could each of your questions be investigated?"],
    "s.8.3.learnDetail.3": ["If you investigate the effect of indigestion powders on stomach acid, you cannot use your own stomach acid.","You have to use a model instead, such as a beaker of acid."],
    "s.8.3.learnDetail.4": ["How will I make my test fair?","What will I measure? What will I do to stay safe? What equipment do I need?","What will I change in the investigation and what will I keep the same?"],
    "s.8.3.learnDetail.5": ["How will you know when the powder has neutralised the acid?","What will you see happen?","How will you carry out the investigation, and how will you record the results?"],
    "s.8.3.learnDetail.6": ["In the same group, choose one question from the previous activity and plan how to carry it out.","After discussing it, divide up the tasks, for example: equipment list, method, safety, results table…","Each member produces something to put on a large piece of paper to share with the class; you may carry it out once it has been checked for safety.","Self-assessment: how well did your group do? Was the plan safe, would it work, was the test fair?"],
    "s.8.3.learnDetail.7": ["Put 20 cm³ of hydrochloric acid into each of three beakers; the acid has a pH of 1, like the strong acid in your stomach.","Add a few drops of universal indicator to each beaker.","Add the indigestion powder spatula by spatula until the acid is neutralised and the indicator is green.","Do this with each of the powders A, B and C and record the number of spatulas used."],
    "s.8.3.learnDetail.8": ["Results table: A – 10 spatulas; B – 6; C – 24.","The book asks: what should they do to stay safe with pH 1 acid? Which variables are kept the same, which is changed, what is measured?","The book asks: which powder is most effective, which is least effective, and is there enough evidence to be certain?"],
    "s.8.3.learnDetail.9": ["Powder A: 10, 9, 11 spatulas – mean 10.","Powder B: 6, 17, 16 spatulas – mean 13.","Powder C: 24, 23, 25 spatulas – mean 24.","The book asks: which powder is now the most effective? Which result looks 'wrong', why might they have got it, what should they have done, and should it be included in the mean?"],
    "s.8.3.learnDetail.10": ["The book asks: what do the three sets of results tell you about carrying out an investigation?","When you see evidence from someone else's investigation, what do you need to ask?","Goal: word a question so it can be tested, plan an investigation, and look critically at what the results tell you."],
    "s.8.3.extend": ["Names of the variables: independent variable — what you deliberately change (the type of powder); dependent variable — what you measure (the number of spatulas); control variables — what you keep the same (volume and strength of acid, size of spatula…).","A result very different from the others is called an anomalous result: check it, repeat it and leave it out of the mean. Without the 6, B's mean is (17 + 16) ÷ 2 = 16.5 spatulas — so A is actually the most effective powder.","Acid of pH 1 is corrosive: wear safety glasses, wipe up spills at once and wash your hands after the experiment.","Antacids sold in pharmacies usually contain alkaline substances such as magnesium hydroxide, aluminium hydroxide or calcium carbonate."],
    "s.8.3.figcap": ["From a vague question to a testable one","Not real stomach acid – a model: a beaker of acid","Questions to answer when planning an investigation","Marcus and Arun's method","Results of the first try (the book's data)","Three tries for each powder and the book's means – look carefully at every point"],
    "s.8.2.learn": ["Acids and alkalis cancel each other out: when you mix them they react and make a neutral solution – this is **neutralisation**.","The amount of acid must be just right: too much makes the liquid acidic, too little leaves it alkaline.","A **burette** lets you neutralise an alkali very accurately.","As acid is added from the burette, the pH in the flask falls from about 13 to 7 and then to about 6.","Neutralisation is a chemical reaction: hydrochloric acid + sodium hydroxide → sodium chloride + water.","The 'rainbow neutralisation' activity shows the different colours of universal indicator.","After a few days the tube shows bands of colour from strongly acidic at the top to strongly alkaline at the bottom.","Your stomach makes acid to **digest** food; too much acid gives **indigestion**, which is treated with alkaline medicines.","Toothpaste contains alkali that helps neutralise the acid made by **bacteria** in your mouth.","Acid rain changes the pH of lakes, rivers and ponds; some countries drop alkalis into lakes to neutralise the acid.","Plants do not grow well in very acidic soil; farmers spread lime to neutralise the acid.","Investigation: test the pH of a soil sample by filtering it and adding universal indicator to the **filtrate**."],
    "s.8.2.learnDetail.0": ["This is another chemical change.","Neutrality is also a chemical property of a substance.","The book asks: what colour is universal indicator in a neutral solution? What sort of reaction happens when an acid and an alkali are mixed?"],
    "s.8.2.learnDetail.1": ["Too much acid added to an alkali → an acidic liquid.","Too little acid → it stays an alkaline liquid.","Adding the acid very slowly, a few drops at a time, makes it easier to judge exactly when it becomes neutral."],
    "s.8.2.learnDetail.2": ["A burette is a long tube with a scale (0 cm³ at the top, 50 cm³ at the bottom) and a tap at the bottom.","Add universal indicator to the alkali in a conical flask under the burette.","Add the acid slowly, and shake the flask each time some acid is added."],
    "s.8.2.learnDetail.3": ["Diagram 1: no acid added yet, the pH in the flask is about 13; as acid is added the pH becomes lower.","Diagram 2: 25 cm³ of acid added, the pH in the flask is 7 – the liquid is neutral; the acid has reacted with the alkali and **neutralised** it.","Diagram 3: a little more acid added (to 28 cm³), the pH is about 6 – the liquid is weakly acidic."],
    "s.8.2.learnDetail.4": ["When this happens there is a chemical reaction and new substances are formed.","Hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide (an alkali) are the reactants.","The products that form are sodium chloride and water."],
    "s.8.2.learnDetail.5": ["You need: the apparatus shown – a test tube, a clamp stand, a crystal of washing soda, water, universal indicator and hydrochloric acid. Safety: wear safety glasses.","Steps: fix a test tube in a clamp stand where it will not be moved; put a crystal of washing soda at the bottom; carefully add water until about two-thirds full; add a few drops of universal indicator.","Carefully pour some acid on the top, do not shake the tube; leave it to stand for a few days.","The book asks: the pH at the top and at the bottom; the most alkaline part; why the tube must be kept still; which hazard symbols are on the equipment."],
    "s.8.2.learnDetail.6": ["At the top: the acid turns the indicator red – strongly acidic. The acid particles gradually move down and mix with more water, so the indicator turns yellow – more weakly acidic.","In the middle: the acid and the washing soda solution mix, react together and neutralise each other.","At the bottom: the washing soda dissolves in the water around it; the indicator is purple or dark blue – washing soda is a strong alkali.","The washing soda particles gradually move up and mix with more water, so the indicator turns a lighter blue – more weakly alkaline.","Self-assessment: how successful was your rainbow, and what could you have done to improve it?"],
    "s.8.2.learnDetail.7": ["Your stomach produces hydrochloric acid; the acid gives the stomach the right conditions to digest food.","When your stomach produces too much acid you have indigestion, which can be very uncomfortable.","Many medicines can help; they are all alkalis and neutralise the acid. They are sometimes called antacids."],
    "s.8.2.learnDetail.8": ["There are millions of bacteria in your mouth; they feed on the pieces of food left on your teeth.","The bacteria produce acid when they feed; the acid damages your teeth and makes them **decay**.","Toothpaste contains alkali, which helps to neutralise the acid.","The book asks: why is toothpaste alkaline? Where does the acid in your mouth come from?"],
    "s.8.2.learnDetail.9": ["In some parts of the world there are harmful chemicals in the air that make the rain acidic.","Acid rain damages trees and changes the pH of lakes, rivers and ponds.","The plants and animals in the lakes cannot live in acid conditions, so some countries drop alkalis into the lakes to neutralise the acid.","The book asks: why is an alkaline substance dropped into lakes in some countries?"],
    "s.8.2.learnDetail.10": ["In some areas the soil is very acidic and plants do not grow well.","Farmers spread lime on the soil to neutralise the acid, so that the plants can grow better.","The book asks: what do farmers spread onto acidic soil, and why?"],
    "s.8.2.learnDetail.11": ["You need: 2 test tubes, a beaker of water, a filter funnel, filter paper, universal indicator and a sample of soil.","Steps: put a soil sample in a test tube and add some water; shake the tube; filter the mixture.","Add a few drops of universal indicator to the filtrate – the liquid that comes through the filter paper; record your results.","The book asks: use books or the internet to find out what plants grow well in this type of soil."],
    "s.8.2.extend": ["Universal indicator colour scale: red (strong acid, pH 1–2) → orange, yellow (weak acid) → green (neutral, pH 7) → blue (weak alkali) → purple (strong alkali, pH 13–14).","An acid reacting with an alkali always makes a salt and water; the salt's name comes from the metal in the alkali and the ending from the acid (hydrochloric → chloride, sulfuric → sulfate).","In Vietnam, the acid sulfate soils of the Mekong Delta are often limed; shrimp and fish farmers also spread lime in their ponds to raise the pH of the water.","The rainbow tube must be kept still because shaking would mix the acid and alkali at once, so no bands could form; left still, the particles spread (diffuse) only very slowly."],
    "s.8.2.figcap": ["The amount of acid decides the result: too little, just enough or too much","Adding acid from a burette: 0 → 25 → 28 cm³, and the indicator colour in the flask changes","The book's three pH readings: the pH falls as acid is added, exactly 7 after 25 cm³","The word equation for the neutralisation in the book","Rainbow neutralisation after a few days: the acid spreads down, the alkali spreads up","Neutralisation in everyday life: acid meets alkali and is neutralised","Testing soil pH: add water, shake, filter, add indicator to the filtrate"],
    "s.8.1.learn": ["Physical properties tell you a substance's colour, its state, its boiling or melting temperature and whether it is heavy or light.","Chemical properties tell you how acidic or alkaline a substance is, what it **reacts** with and how readily.","A physical change makes no new substance; a chemical change makes new substances.","When new substances form, a **chemical reaction** has taken place: the **reactants** change into the **products**.","In some reactions one substance breaks apart into new substances; chemical reactions happen everywhere.","Burning is a chemical reaction: the substance reacts with the oxygen in the air.","Burning magnesium ribbon makes a new product: magnesium oxide.","The product has very different properties from the reactants.","Some substances react very violently with water; some do not react with water at all.","Magnesium reacts with hydrochloric acid and gives off bubbles of hydrogen.","Test for hydrogen with a lighted splint: hydrogen burns with a squeaky pop.","Investigation: react metals with water and with acid to find out about their chemical properties."],
    "s.8.1.learnDetail.0": ["Example physical properties of iron: a grey, heavy solid with a melting point of 1538 °C.","One physical property of hydrochloric acid: it is a colourless liquid."],
    "s.8.1.learnDetail.1": ["Chemical properties include: how acidic or alkaline it is; how it reacts with water, acids or metals; how readily it reacts.","Iron **combines** with sulfur when heated to form iron sulfide.","Iron combines with oxygen to form iron oxide, or rust.","One chemical property of hydrochloric acid: it has a pH of 2."],
    "s.8.1.learnDetail.2": ["When liquid water freezes it only changes state; after the change it is still the same substance (water).","Heating iron and sulfur together makes a new substance – a compound called iron sulfide.","The book asks: making toast, melting chocolate, fireworks going off, ice melting, coal burning, a copper roof turning green – physical change or chemical reaction, and why?"],
    "s.8.1.learnDetail.3": ["In iron + sulfur → iron sulfide, the iron atoms have combined and bonded with the sulfur atoms.","The reactants are the substances that react together: here, iron and sulfur.","The products are the new substances made; this reaction has only one product, iron sulfide."],
    "s.8.1.learnDetail.4": ["Example: water can be split apart to form oxygen and hydrogen (water → oxygen + hydrogen).","Chemical reactions happen inside plants when they grow and when they decay.","Chemical reactions inside your body keep you alive, for example when you digest food."],
    "s.8.1.learnDetail.5": ["Sometimes ashes are left; the ashes contain new substances, which are oxides.","Charcoal is made of the element carbon; when carbon burns it combines with oxygen in the air to make the gas carbon dioxide.","Burning magnesium metal makes a white powder, magnesium oxide; the reactants are magnesium and oxygen, the product is magnesium oxide.","Burning in air to form magnesium oxide is a chemical property of magnesium."],
    "s.8.1.learnDetail.6": ["You need: safety glasses, a Bunsen burner, a heat-proof mat, tongs and a piece of magnesium ribbon.","Safety: wear safety glasses; while the magnesium is burning do not look directly at the flame, because the very bright light could harm your eyes.","Steps: set up the Bunsen burner on the heat-proof mat; hold a small piece of magnesium in the tongs; hold the tongs at arm's length and put the magnesium in the flame; once it catches fire, remove it from the flame.","The book asks: describe what happens to the ribbon, what has been formed, name the reactants and list all the safety precautions.","Not all metals have the same chemical properties as magnesium: you could hold copper, zinc or iron in the Bunsen flame and record what happens."],
    "s.8.1.learnDetail.7": ["Magnesium: element, solid, soft, shiny, malleable, conducts electricity, melts at 651 °C.","Oxygen: element, gas, colourless, no smell, does not conduct electricity, melts at −214 °C.","Magnesium oxide: compound, solid, white, powdery, does not conduct electricity, melts at 2800 °C.","The book asks: compare the three melting points, and find one similarity between magnesium oxide and one of the reactants."],
    "s.8.1.learnDetail.8": ["Potassium (a metal) is very soft and can be cut with a knife – a physical property.","Potassium is so reactive that it is stored under oil so it cannot react with the water vapour in the air – a chemical property.","A very small piece in a large trough of water gives off hydrogen; the reaction produces so much heat that the gas burns.","Safety: you must not do this yourself; if your teacher demonstrates it you must wear safety glasses and there must be a safety screen in front of the trough."],
    "s.8.1.learnDetail.9": ["Put magnesium in hydrochloric acid and bubbles of gas are given off: the magnesium has reacted with the acid and formed new substances.","magnesium + hydrochloric acid → magnesium chloride + hydrogen; this is a chemical property of magnesium.","Bubbles tell you that a gas is being produced, but not what type of gas it is."],
    "s.8.1.learnDetail.10": ["To test: light a splint and put it at the mouth of the test tube.","Keep your finger over the end of the tube until the last moment, or there will be no hydrogen left to test, because hydrogen is lighter than air.","The pop is the hydrogen reacting with oxygen in the air to form water.","To get more hydrogen you can collect it over water: acid goes through a thistle funnel into a conical flask of metal, and the gas goes along a delivery tube into an upturned measuring cylinder on a bee-hive shelf in a trough of water."],
    "s.8.1.learnDetail.11": ["You need: safety glasses, test tubes, a test tube rack, water, hydrochloric acid and small pieces of metals such as magnesium, iron, copper and zinc. Safety: wear safety glasses and pay attention to hazard warning labels.","Part 1 (water): one metal per tube; add water to each in turn until half full; record your observations in a table.","Part 2 (acid): one metal per tube; add hydrochloric acid to each in turn until half full; if you see bubbles, test for hydrogen; record your observations in a table.","For each reaction with acid, write down the reactants and the products; say what safety precautions you took and how you tested for hydrogen.","The book asks: two chemical and two physical properties of magnesium; the products when zinc reacts with hydrochloric acid."],
    "s.8.1.extend": ["Many metals react with acids in the same way: metal + acid → salt + hydrogen, for example zinc + hydrochloric acid → zinc chloride + hydrogen. They differ in how readily: magnesium fizzes fast, iron more slowly, copper hardly reacts with dilute acid.","In a chemical reaction atoms are not lost or created, only rearranged, so the total mass stays the same — you will meet this again as the law of conservation of mass (Natural Science 8).","Around you: toasting bread, burning a honeycomb coal briquette, an iron nail rusting and a bronze statue turning green are chemical changes; ice melting and chocolate melting are physical changes.","The dazzling white light of burning magnesium was once used in old camera flashes and in flares."],
    "s.8.1.figcap": ["A physical change only changes the state; a chemical change makes new substances","Reactants on the left of the arrow, products on the right – four reactions from this topic","Burning magnesium: the grey metal burns dazzlingly and becomes a white powder","Melting points (the book's data): the product is very different from the reactants","Testing for hydrogen: keep the tube closed until the last moment, then bring a lighted splint to its mouth","Getting more hydrogen by collecting the gas over water"],
    "g.0.def": "A process in which the starting substances change into new substances.",
    "g.1.def": "A substance present at the start that is changed in the reaction.",
    "g.2.def": "A new substance made in the reaction.",
    "g.3.def": "The reaction between an acid and an alkali that makes a neutral solution (pH 7).",
    "g.4.def": "A long glass tube with a scale and a tap at the bottom, used to add a liquid very accurately.",
    "g.5.def": "The liquid that passes through the filter paper.",
    "g.6.def": "A factor that can change in an experiment.",
    "g.7.def": "An insoluble solid formed when two solutions are mixed.",
    "g.8.def": "A solution of calcium hydroxide; it turns cloudy with carbon dioxide.",
    "g.9.def": "Discomfort when the stomach makes too much acid.",
    "ex.8.1.q": "Lan drops a piece of zinc into a test tube of hydrochloric acid and sees bubbles of gas. Write the word equation and say which gas it is.",
    "ex.8.1.steps": [
      "Bubbles mean the reaction makes a gas — a new substance has formed, so this is a chemical reaction.",
      "The pattern: metal + acid → salt + hydrogen.",
      "The salt's name is the metal's name + the ending from the acid: hydrochloric acid gives 'chloride' → zinc chloride.",
      "Word equation: zinc + hydrochloric acid → zinc chloride + hydrogen. The gas is hydrogen; check it with a lighted splint → squeaky pop."
    ],
    "ex.8.1.prompts": [
      "What do bubbles tell you: is there a new substance, or just a change of state?",
      "You have met magnesium reacting with acid. What are the products of metal + acid usually?",
      "What ending does the name of a salt made from hydrochloric acid have?",
      "Put it together as a word equation. Will you test the gas with a lighted splint or a glowing one?"
    ],
    "ex.8.1.goal": "Use the pattern 'metal + acid → salt + hydrogen' to write a word equation and choose the right gas test.",
    "ex.8.1.twist": "If you use magnesium and sulfuric acid instead, what is the name of the salt formed?",
    "ex.8.2.q": "A burette containing hydrochloric acid reads 4.0 cm³ at the start. The tap is opened so acid drips into a flask of sodium hydroxide with universal indicator; when the solution just turns green, the reading is 29.5 cm³. How many cm³ of acid were used?",
    "ex.8.2.steps": [
      "Green means pH 7: the solution has just become neutral, which is where you stop.",
      "The 0 mark of a burette is at the top; as acid runs out, the level falls, so the reading goes up.",
      "Volume added = final reading − first reading = 29.5 − 4.0.",
      "= 25.5 cm³ of acid."
    ],
    "ex.8.2.prompts": [
      "Green universal indicator matches which pH?",
      "On a burette, is 0 at the top or the bottom? As acid runs out, does the reading go up or down?",
      "To find how much acid ran out, what calculation do you do with the two readings?",
      "How many cm³ is that?"
    ],
    "ex.8.2.goal": "Read a burette correctly and work out the volume of acid needed to exactly neutralise an amount of alkali.",
    "ex.8.2.twist": "If the flask held twice as much sodium hydroxide (same concentration), how many cm³ of acid would be needed?",
    "ex.8.3.q": "Minh's group measured how many spatulas of powder P were needed to neutralise 20 cm³ of acid, four times: 12, 11, 19, 13. Find the anomalous result, then work out a sensible mean.",
    "ex.8.3.steps": [
      "12, 11 and 13 are close together; 19 is far away from them, so it is anomalous.",
      "It could come from miscounting, unevenly filled spatulas or the wrong acid — so leave 19 out.",
      "Mean = (12 + 11 + 13) ÷ 3 = 36 ÷ 3.",
      "= 12 spatulas."
    ],
    "ex.8.3.prompts": [
      "Of the four numbers, which one is very different from the rest?",
      "Why might one measurement be so far off? Should it go into the mean?",
      "To find the mean of the remaining numbers, you add them and divide by what?",
      "How many spatulas is that?"
    ],
    "ex.8.3.goal": "Spot an anomalous result and work out the mean from the reliable results only.",
    "ex.8.3.twist": "If you included the 19 anyway, what would the mean be? (give a decimal)",
    "ex.8.4.q": "Three test tubes contain oxygen, hydrogen and carbon dioxide, but the labels are lost. Test results: tube 1 turns limewater cloudy; tube 2 relights a glowing splint; tube 3 gives a squeaky pop with a lighted splint. Which gas is in tube 2?",
    "ex.8.4.steps": [
      "Cloudy limewater is the sign of carbon dioxide → tube 1 is carbon dioxide.",
      "A squeaky pop with a lighted splint is the sign of hydrogen → tube 3 is hydrogen.",
      "A glowing splint relights only when there is a lot of oxygen (the gas that keeps things burning).",
      "So tube 2 contains oxygen."
    ],
    "ex.8.4.prompts": [
      "Which gas is the limewater test for?",
      "A squeaky pop is the sign of which gas?",
      "Which gas makes a glowing splint burst into flame again?",
      "So which gas is in tube 2?"
    ],
    "ex.8.4.goal": "Use the three gas tests (lighted splint, glowing splint, limewater) to identify each gas.",
    "ex.8.4.twist": "There is a tube 4: limewater hardly changes, a glowing splint does not relight and there is no pop. What could tube 4 contain?",
    "u.challenge.title": "Vietnam Challenge",
    "u.challenge.intro": "The cave door opens only when you can explain the chemistry around you: from a jar of pickled mustard greens and a bee sting to a field of acidic soil.",
    "q.8.1-01.hints": ["How is a chemical change different from a physical change: is a new substance made?", "Which three cases are only a change of state (solid → liquid, liquid → gas)?", "Is the brown layer on the toast still the same bread as before?"],
    "q.8.1-01.explain": "Toasting bread makes a new brown substance with a smell, and it cannot be undone — that is a chemical reaction. Ice melting, chocolate melting and water boiling are only changes of state (physical changes).",
    "q.8.1-02.hints": ["Physical properties are what you can observe or measure without turning the substance into something else.", "Do rusting and making iron sulfide produce new substances?", "Which kind of property are colour, melting point and state?"],
    "q.8.1-02.explain": "Grey colour, a melting point of 1538 °C and being solid at room temperature are physical properties. Rusting and combining with sulfur are chemical properties because iron turns into new substances.",
    "q.8.1-03.hints": ["When something burns in air, which gas does it combine with?", "A compound of an element with oxygen is called '… oxide'.", "Put the metal's name together with 'oxide'."],
    "q.8.1-03.explain": "Magnesium combines with oxygen to form magnesium oxide (a white powder): magnesium + oxygen → magnesium oxide.",
    "q.8.1-04.hints": ["Vinegar and baking powder also fizz. Is that gas hydrogen?", "By looking at bubbles, can you tell the name of the gas?", "To find out which gas it is, what else must you do?"],
    "q.8.1-04.explain": "A common mistake is thinking that any fizzing means hydrogen. Bubbles only show that a gas is made; it could be hydrogen, carbon dioxide, oxygen… You must use a test (splint, limewater) to identify it.",
    "q.8.1-05.hints": ["Hydrogen is a gas that burns. Do you need a lighted splint or a glowing one?", "Which test is for oxygen and which is for carbon dioxide?", "What sound does hydrogen make when it meets a flame?"],
    "q.8.1-05.explain": "Hydrogen burns with a squeaky pop with a lighted splint. A glowing splint relighting is the test for oxygen; limewater turning cloudy is the test for carbon dioxide.",
    "q.8.1-06.howto": "Find the difference between the melting point of the product (magnesium oxide) and the melting point of the metal (magnesium).",
    "q.8.1-06.hints": ["Which two numbers in the table do you need to compare?", "Is 'how much higher' an addition or a subtraction?", "Take 651 away from 2800."],
    "q.8.1-06.explain": "2800 − 651 = 2149 °C. The product has very different properties from the reactants — a sign that a new substance has formed.",
    "q.8.1-07.hints": ["What does a burning magnesium flame look like?", "What damages human eyes: very dazzling light or smoke?", "Apart from safety glasses, how do you hold the tongs safely?"],
    "q.8.1-07.explain": "Burning magnesium gives an extremely bright white light that can harm your eyes. So wear safety glasses, hold the tongs at arm's length and do not look straight at the flame.",
    "q.8.1-08.hints": ["Which metal reacts even with cold water? Is it the most or the least reactive?", "In acid, what do fast bubbles and slow bubbles tell you about how readily the metal reacts?", "Where does a metal that reacts with neither water nor acid go?"],
    "q.8.1-08.explain": "Z reacts even with cold water, so it is the most reactive; W fizzes fast in acid; Y fizzes slowly; X does not react (like copper). Order: Z, W, Y, X.",
    "q.8.1-08.why.q": "Which of the metals you have studied could metal X be?",
    "q.8.1-08.why.choices": ["Copper", "Potassium", "Magnesium"],
    "q.8.1-09.hints": ["Is the green layer still copper, or a different substance?", "Copper is exposed to air and water vapour for many years. What does that suggest?", "Does a long or short time decide whether it is a chemical reaction?"],
    "q.8.1-09.explain": "The green layer is a new substance made when copper reacts with substances in the air — a chemical change. The reason is not that it took a long time or changed colour, but that a new substance formed.",
    "q.8.1-10.hints": ["If the tube were left open, where would the hydrogen go?", "Does that gas tend to move up or down when the tube is opened?", "If all the gas escaped, what would you hear when you brought the splint near?"],
    "q.8.1-10.explain": "Hydrogen is lighter than air, so it rises and escapes from the tube. Keeping the tube closed until the last moment keeps enough hydrogen for the pop.",
    "q.8.2-01.hints": ["What pH does a neutral solution have?", "pH 7 is in the middle of the pH scale. Which colour lies between red and purple?", "Red is strong acid, purple is strong alkali. Which colour is left?"],
    "q.8.2-01.explain": "A neutral solution (pH 7) turns universal indicator green.",
    "q.8.2-02.hints": ["The product has pH 7. What do we call such a solution?", "Which reaction name comes from the word 'neutral'?", "In English: 'neutral' → 'neutral…'"],
    "q.8.2-02.explain": "It is neutralisation: acid + alkali → salt + water, and the solution formed is neutral.",
    "q.8.2-03.hints": ["On a burette, 0 is at the very top; the numbers increase as you go down.", "How many cm³ is each small mark? Count down from the 10 mark.", "Read at the bottom of the curved liquid surface."],
    "q.8.2-03.explain": "Each small mark is 1 cm³. The bottom of the meniscus is 8 marks below the 10 mark → 18 cm³.",
    "q.8.2-04.hints": ["Is the final reading bigger or smaller than the first? Why?", "The volume that ran out is the difference between which two readings?", "Work out 27.0 − 3.5."],
    "q.8.2-04.explain": "Volume of acid added = 27.0 − 3.5 = 23.5 cm³.",
    "q.8.2-05.hints": ["After 20 cm³ the acid and alkali have exactly used each other up. Is there any alkali left for the extra 3 cm³ of acid to react with?", "Does extra acid make the pH go above or below 7?", "With only a little extra acid, is the solution a strong or a weak acid?"],
    "q.8.2-05.explain": "A common mistake is thinking that once it is neutral, adding any amount of acid keeps it at pH 7. In fact the extra acid has no alkali left to neutralise it, so the solution becomes weakly acidic (about pH 6). That is why you add drop by drop near the end point.",
    "q.8.2-06.hints": ["Bacteria in your mouth feed on bits of food and make what substance?", "What does acid do to tooth enamel?", "What reaction happens when an alkali meets an acid?"],
    "q.8.2-06.explain": "Bacteria feed on bits of food and make acid that causes tooth decay. The alkali in toothpaste neutralises that acid.",
    "q.8.2-07.hints": ["Is pH 5 acidic, neutral or alkaline?", "To bring the pH closer to 7, do you need to add an acid or an alkali?", "What do Vietnamese farmers often spread on acidic rice fields?"],
    "q.8.2-07.explain": "Soil at pH 5 is acidic. Spreading lime (an alkali) neutralises the acid and brings the pH closer to 7, so the crops grow well.",
    "q.8.2-08.hints": ["When filtering, which part stays on the filter paper?", "Which part runs down into the test tube below?", "Do you add the indicator to the clear part or the muddy part?"],
    "q.8.2-08.explain": "The filtrate is the liquid that passes through the filter paper. The soil stays on the paper. Add indicator to the clear filtrate so the colour is easy to see.",
    "q.8.2-09.hints": ["Where is the alkali at the start, and where is the acid?", "The further from the alkali, is it diluted and neutralised more or less?", "Where does the dark purple colour of the indicator appear?"],
    "q.8.2-09.explain": "The washing soda dissolves at the bottom, so the bottom of the tube is the most alkaline (purple). The acid is at the top (red). In the middle the acid and alkali meet and neutralise (yellow/green) — making a 'rainbow'.",
    "q.8.2-09.why.q": "Why must the tube be left still, without shaking?",
    "q.8.2-09.why.choices": ["If you shake it, the acid and alkali mix evenly and there is only one colour", "Shaking would make the washing soda explode", "Shaking makes the indicator lose its colour for ever"],
    "q.8.2-10.hints": ["How many cm³ of acid does 1 cm³ of alkali need? (20 ÷ 25)", "The amount of acid is proportional to the amount of alkali. How many times bigger is 60 cm³ than 25 cm³?", "Multiply 20 by 60 ÷ 25."],
    "q.8.2-10.explain": "Each cm³ of alkali needs 20 ÷ 25 = 0.8 cm³ of acid. For 60 cm³ of alkali: 60 × 0.8 = 48 cm³ of acid.",
    "q.8.3-01.hints": ["A testable question must have something you can measure as a number. Which one does?", "Can 'best' or 'nicest' be measured with equipment?", "Which question says clearly what to measure and how to compare?"],
    "q.8.3-01.explain": "Only the first question names a measurable quantity (the number of spatulas of powder needed to neutralise 20 cm³ of acid). The others use vague words like 'best' or 'nicest'.",
    "q.8.3-02.hints": ["The independent variable is what you deliberately change between tries.", "Between tries, what does Marcus change?", "Is the number of spatulas something you choose first or something you measure?"],
    "q.8.3-02.explain": "Marcus deliberately changes the type of powder (A, B, C) — that is the independent variable. The number of spatulas is the dependent variable (measured).",
    "q.8.3-03.hints": ["The dependent variable is the result you measure or count.", "What does Marcus count until the indicator changes colour?", "Is the volume of acid kept the same or changed?"],
    "q.8.3-03.explain": "Marcus counts the spatulas of powder needed to turn the indicator green — that is the dependent variable.",
    "q.8.3-04.hints": ["Control variables are everything that could affect the result but that you do not want to change.", "The type of powder is what is changed, and the number of spatulas is what is measured. So what is left?", "If the spatulas were different sizes, could the 'number of spatulas' still be compared?"],
    "q.8.3-04.explain": "Keep the same: volume of acid, strength (pH) of acid, number of drops of indicator, size of spatula. The type of powder is the independent variable; the number of spatulas is the dependent variable.",
    "q.8.3-05.hints": ["What if the count for B was a mistake?", "How can you spot an anomalous result?", "Is one measurement enough to be certain?"],
    "q.8.3-05.explain": "A common mistake is believing one measurement is enough. One result can be wrong because of miscounting or unevenly filled spatulas. Repeat at least 3 times, look for anomalous results, then conclude.",
    "q.8.3-06.hints": ["Mean = total of the measurements ÷ number of measurements.", "What is 14 + 17 + 14?", "Divide the total by 3."],
    "q.8.3-06.explain": "(14 + 17 + 14) ÷ 3 = 45 ÷ 3 = 15 spatulas.",
    "q.8.3-07.hints": ["For each powder, are the three results close together?", "In which row is one number far away from the other two?", "A difference of 1 is normal; what about a difference of 7–8?"],
    "q.8.3-07.explain": "The results for A and C differ by only 1. For B, 12 and 13 are close but 5 is far off → 5 is the anomalous result.",
    "q.8.3-08.howto": "Leave out the one result that does not fit, then find the mean of the results that are left.",
    "q.8.3-08.hints": ["Which result for B is left out?", "How many results are left? What is their total?", "Divide the total by the number of results left."],
    "q.8.3-08.explain": "Leave out 5. Mean = (12 + 13) ÷ 2 = 12.5 spatulas. Including 5 would give 10 — misleading, making B look better than it really is.",
    "q.8.3-09.hints": ["Is an effective medicine one that needs a lot or a little powder to neutralise the same acid?", "The fewer spatulas, does each spatula neutralise more or less acid?", "Find the powder with the smallest mean."],
    "q.8.3-09.explain": "A common mistake is thinking the biggest number is the best. Here needing the least powder is the most effective, so A is the most effective and C the least.",
    "q.8.3-10.hints": ["To compare 'number of scoops', how much powder must each scoop hold?", "Do a teaspoon and a small spatula hold the same amount of powder?", "If just one control variable changes, is the test still fair?"],
    "q.8.3-10.explain": "Not fair: a teaspoon holds more powder than a small spatula, so the numbers of scoops cannot be compared. Use the same spatula each time (or weigh the powder).",
    "q.8.4-01.hints": ["Does the carbon dioxide test use a splint?", "Which solution is 'limewater'?", "What do you see when carbon dioxide is bubbled through limewater?"],
    "q.8.4-01.explain": "Carbon dioxide turns limewater cloudy (it makes white calcium carbonate). A glowing splint is the test for oxygen; a squeaky pop is the test for hydrogen.",
    "q.8.4-02.hints": ["A glowing splint relighting needs a lot of which gas?", "Which gas keeps things burning?", "Hydrogen gives a different sign: what is it?"],
    "q.8.4-02.explain": "Oxygen keeps things burning, so it makes a glowing splint burst into flame again.",
    "q.8.4-03.hints": ["At first both are clear solutions. What appears after mixing?", "An insoluble solid formed from two solutions has its own special name.", "In Vietnamese it is 'kết …'."],
    "q.8.4-03.explain": "An insoluble solid formed when two solutions are mixed is a precipitate, for example silver chloride when silver nitrate is mixed with calcium chloride.",
    "q.8.4-04.hints": ["Which signs of a chemical reaction have you learned: gas, colour, heat, precipitate…?", "Does sugar dissolving in water make a new substance? If you evaporate the water, what do you get back?", "Is ice melting a change of state or a new substance?"],
    "q.8.4-04.explain": "A colour change (black → blue), a gas given off and heat produced are signs of a chemical reaction. Sugar dissolving and ice melting are physical changes with no new substance.",
    "q.8.4-05.hints": ["After it cools down, does the chocolate set into chocolate again?", "Is any new substance made?", "Does heating always cause a chemical reaction?"],
    "q.8.4-05.explain": "A common mistake is thinking that every change you can see is a chemical reaction. The chocolate only melts (a change of state) and sets again when cooled; no new substance forms → physical change.",
    "q.8.4-06.hints": ["Which metal is in limewater?", "Carbon dioxide joins with the metal to make a compound ending in '… carbonate'.", "Put the metal's name together with 'carbonate'."],
    "q.8.4-06.explain": "calcium hydroxide + carbon dioxide → calcium carbonate + water. Calcium carbonate (found in limestone) is an insoluble white solid that makes the limewater cloudy.",
    "q.8.4-07.hints": ["Is the blue solution a starting substance or a new one?", "What are the products of copper oxide + sulfuric acid?", "What is the safety point when heating this solution?"],
    "q.8.4-07.explain": "The colour change from black to blue shows that copper sulfate (a new substance) has formed. Heat gently only, do not boil, because harmful fumes would be given off.",
    "q.8.4-08.hints": ["For each experiment, look for signs: gas, colour change, heat, precipitate.", "Which experiment shows none of these signs?", "Does salt dissolving in water make a new substance? If you evaporate the water, what do you get back?"],
    "q.8.4-08.explain": "P is only dissolving (a physical change) — evaporating the water gives back the salt. Q gives off a gas; R changes colour and gets warm (neutralisation); S gives off a gas and gets hot → all chemical reactions.",
    "q.8.4-09.hints": ["In a chemical reaction, are atoms lost?", "What are the products of magnesium + hydrochloric acid?", "Is magnesium chloride a solid lump or dissolved in the solution?"],
    "q.8.4-09.explain": "A common mistake is thinking a substance that 'disappears' has been destroyed. The magnesium atoms join with chlorine to make magnesium chloride, which dissolves in the solution so you cannot see it; the gas given off is hydrogen.",
    "q.8.4-10.hints": ["Potassium + water makes which gas?", "Why does that gas catch fire without a splint?", "With such a violent reaction, should learners do it themselves?"],
    "q.8.4-10.explain": "Potassium reacts with water to make hydrogen and gives out so much heat that the hydrogen catches fire (gas + heat are signs of a reaction). It is very violent, so only the teacher does it, behind a safety screen and with safety glasses.",
    "q.ch-01.q": "Consider these: (1) ice melting in a glass of iced tea; (2) an iron nail left out in the rain for a long time goes rusty; (3) pickled mustard greens ferment and taste sour; (4) the alcohol in a spirit lamp evaporates when the cap is left off; (5) burning a honeycomb coal briquette for cooking. Which of these are chemical changes?",
    "q.ch-01.hints": ["For each one ask: is a new substance made?", "Are ice melting and alcohol evaporating changes of state or new substances?", "Are rust, the sour taste of the pickles and the coal ash new substances?"],
    "q.ch-01.explain": "(2) makes rust, (3) bacteria make lactic acid which tastes sour, (5) burning coal makes carbon dioxide and ash → chemical. (1) and (4) are only changes of state → physical.",
    "q.ch-02.q": "The venom of bees and fire ants contains an acid (formic acid) that causes a burning pain. Which is the most sensible thing to dab on as first aid?",
    "q.ch-02.choices": ["Dilute limewater or a little toothpaste (mildly alkaline)", "Vinegar, because it is acidic and soothes the pain", "Lime juice, because lime disinfects the sting very well", "Strong salt water, because salt neutralises every substance"],
    "q.ch-02.hints": ["To reduce the effect of an acid, what kind of substance do you need?", "Are vinegar and lime juice acidic or alkaline?", "Why choose a mild alkali rather than a strong one to put on skin?"],
    "q.ch-02.explain": "The acid in the venom is neutralised by a mildly alkaline substance such as dilute limewater or toothpaste. Vinegar and lime juice are acids and make it worse; table salt is neutral and does not neutralise acid. A strong alkali would burn the skin.",
    "q.ch-03.howto": "Work out the volume of acid needed, then say the smallest number of times the 50 cm³ burette must be filled (enter a whole number).",
    "q.ch-03.q": "In the laboratory, 10 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution is exactly neutralised by 8 cm³ of hydrochloric acid. Ha needs to neutralise 250 cm³ of the same sodium hydroxide solution with that acid, using a burette that holds at most 50 cm³. What is the smallest number of times Ha must fill the burette?",
    "q.ch-03.hints": ["The amount of acid is proportional to the amount of alkali. How many times bigger is 250 cm³ than 10 cm³?", "So how many cm³ of acid are needed?", "Each filling gives 50 cm³. Divide, and round up if needed."],
    "q.ch-03.explain": "250 ÷ 10 = 25 times → 25 × 8 = 200 cm³ of acid is needed. Each filling gives 50 cm³, so 200 ÷ 50 = 4 fillings.",
    "q.ch-04.q": "In the countryside, a basin of limewater is left out in the yard. A few days later a thin white crust forms on the surface. Which explanation is correct?",
    "q.ch-04.choices": ["Calcium hydroxide reacts with carbon dioxide in the air to form insoluble calcium carbonate", "The water slowly evaporates, so the lime freezes into a crust like ice", "Dust from the air falls and floats as a white layer on the water", "Oxygen in the air reacts with the limewater to make white table salt"],
    "q.ch-04.hints": ["Air contains a small amount of which gas that turns limewater cloudy?", "What is the white solid product of that reaction called?", "Why does the crust appear only on the surface, where it touches the air?"],
    "q.ch-04.explain": "Air contains carbon dioxide: calcium hydroxide + carbon dioxide → calcium carbonate + water. The insoluble calcium carbonate forms a white crust at the surface — where the water touches the air.",
    "q.ch-05.q": "Magnesium reacts completely with excess hydrochloric acid. 1.2 g of magnesium gives 1200 cm³ of hydrogen, and the volume of gas is proportional to the mass of magnesium. How many grams of magnesium are needed to collect 3000 cm³ of hydrogen?",
    "q.ch-05.hints": ["How many cm³ of hydrogen does 1 g of magnesium give?", "How many times bigger is 3000 cm³ than 1200 cm³?", "Multiply 1.2 g by that ratio."],
    "q.ch-05.explain": "1 g of Mg → 1200 ÷ 1.2 = 1000 cm³ of hydrogen. To get 3000 cm³ you need 3000 ÷ 1000 = 3 g of magnesium.",
    "q.ch-06.q": "Three unlabelled bottles contain dilute hydrochloric acid, dilute sodium hydroxide solution and distilled water. Using only ONE reagent, which method identifies all three bottles?",
    "q.ch-06.choices": ["Add universal indicator to a sample from each bottle and compare with the pH colour chart", "Hold a glowing splint at the mouth of each bottle to see if it relights or goes out", "Bubble carbon dioxide through each bottle to see which one turns cloudy", "Taste one drop from each bottle to see if it is sour, bitter or tasteless"],
    "q.ch-06.hints": ["How do these three substances differ: acidic, alkaline or neutral?", "Which reagent gives three different colours depending on pH?", "Are you ever allowed to taste chemicals in the laboratory?"],
    "q.ch-06.explain": "Universal indicator gives different colours: acid → red, alkali → purple/blue, distilled water → green. A splint or CO₂ cannot tell all three apart. Never taste chemicals.",
    "q.ch-07.q": "Choose the FALSE statement.",
    "q.ch-07.choices": ["Whenever bubbles of gas appear, a chemical reaction is happening", "Boiling water bubbles, but it is only a physical change", "Blowing through a straw into limewater turns it cloudy because breath contains carbon dioxide", "When an acid is neutralised by an alkali, the temperature of the solution can rise"],
    "q.ch-07.hints": ["When water boils, what are the bubbles made of? Is there a new substance?", "When you open a bottle of fizzy drink, where do the bubbles come from?", "Which statement uses the word 'whenever' — is there a counter-example?"],
    "q.ch-07.explain": "The first statement is false: boiling water and opening a fizzy drink both make bubbles without any new substance. The other statements are all true.",
    "q.ch-08.q": "A lake has been made acidic by acid rain. It is shaped like a cuboid 200 m long, 50 m wide and 3 m deep on average. Engineers work out that every 1000 m³ of water needs 2 kg of lime to neutralise it. How many kg of lime are needed for the whole lake?",
    "q.ch-08.hints": ["Volume of the lake = length × width × depth = how many m³?", "How many times bigger than 1000 m³ is that volume?", "Each 1000 m³ needs 2 kg of lime. Multiply."],
    "q.ch-08.explain": "Volume = 200 × 50 × 3 = 30 000 m³ = 30 × 1000 m³. Lime needed = 30 × 2 = 60 kg."
  },
  vi: {
    "q.8.1-01.q": "Trường hợp nào sau đây là biến đổi hoá học?",
    "q.8.1-01.choices": ["Nước đá tan trong lòng bàn tay", "Sô-cô-la chảy dưới nắng", "Bánh mì chuyển màu nâu trong máy nướng", "Nước sôi trong ấm"],
    "q.8.1-02.q": "Chọn TẤT CẢ các tính chất vật lí của sắt.",
    "q.8.1-02.choices": ["Có màu xám", "Nóng chảy ở 1538 °C", "Bị gỉ khi phản ứng với oxygen và nước", "Kết hợp với lưu huỳnh khi đun nóng", "Ở thể rắn ở nhiệt độ phòng"],
    "q.8.1-03.q": "Dải magnesium cháy với ngọn lửa trắng sáng chói và để lại bột trắng. Hoàn thành phương trình chữ: magnesium + oxygen → ______",
    "q.8.1-04.q": "Khi cho một loại bột vào chất lỏng thấy có bọt khí. Sami nói: «Cứ có bọt khí là tạo ra hydrogen.» Ai đúng?",
    "q.8.1-04.choices": ["Sami đúng — mọi bọt khí sinh ra trong phản ứng đều là hydrogen", "Không ai biết được — bọt khí không bao giờ cho thấy có phản ứng", "Có khí sinh ra, nhưng cần phép thử để biết đó là khí gì", "Không ai đúng — bọt khí chỉ cho thấy chất lỏng đã bắt đầu sôi"],
    "q.8.1-05.q": "Em thử một chất khí như thế nào để chứng tỏ đó là hydrogen?",
    "q.8.1-05.choices": ["Đưa que đóm đang cháy lại gần ống: khí cháy với tiếng nổ «pốp» nhỏ", "Đưa que đóm còn tàn đỏ vào ống: que đóm bùng cháy sáng trở lại", "Sục khí vào nước vôi trong: nước vôi chuyển sang vẩn đục như sữa", "Nhỏ vài giọt chất chỉ thị vạn năng: chất chỉ thị chuyển màu đỏ"],
    "q.8.1-06.q": "Nhiệt độ nóng chảy (°C): magnesium 651 | oxygen −214 | magnesium oxide 2800. Nhiệt độ nóng chảy của magnesium oxide cao hơn của magnesium bao nhiêu độ?",
    "q.8.1-06.howto": "Tìm hiệu giữa nhiệt độ nóng chảy của sản phẩm (magnesium oxide) và nhiệt độ nóng chảy của kim loại (magnesium).",
    "q.8.1-07.q": "Khi đốt dải magnesium trên ngọn lửa đèn Bunsen, vì sao KHÔNG được nhìn thẳng vào ngọn lửa?",
    "q.8.1-07.choices": ["Ánh sáng quá chói có thể làm hại mắt", "Nó sinh ra khí độc", "Dải magnesium sẽ phát nổ", "Ngọn lửa làm da em trắng ra"],
    "q.8.1-08.q": "Thử bốn kim loại. W: không đổi trong nước, sủi bọt nhanh trong acid. X: không đổi trong cả nước và acid. Y: không đổi trong nước, sủi vài bọt chậm trong acid. Z: sủi bọt trong nước lạnh. Thứ tự nào đi từ kim loại phản ứng MẠNH NHẤT đến YẾU NHẤT?",
    "q.8.1-09.q": "Sau nhiều năm, một mái nhà bằng đồng chuyển sang màu xanh lục. Câu trả lời nào đúng và có lý do tốt nhất?",
    "q.8.1-09.choices": ["Biến đổi hoá học — đồng phản ứng với các khí trong không khí tạo chất mới", "Biến đổi vật lí — chỉ có màu thay đổi, nên mái vẫn chỉ là đồng", "Biến đổi vật lí — chỉ cần sơn lại màu nâu là xong", "Biến đổi hoá học — chắc chắn là hoá học vì mất rất nhiều năm"],
    "q.8.1-10.q": "Khi thử hydrogen, em bịt ngón tay cái lên miệng ống nghiệm cho đến ngay trước khi đưa que đóm đang cháy lại gần. Vì sao?",
    "q.8.1-10.choices": ["Hydrogen nhẹ hơn không khí nên sẽ bay lên thoát ra ngoài", "Hydrogen nặng hơn không khí nên sẽ chìm xuống", "Ngón tay giữ ấm cho ống nghiệm", "Không khí không được trộn với acid"],
    "q.8.2-01.q": "Chất chỉ thị vạn năng có màu gì trong dung dịch trung tính?",
    "q.8.2-01.choices": ["Đỏ", "Xanh lá", "Tím", "Vàng"],
    "q.8.2-02.q": "Acid và kiềm phản ứng tạo ra dung dịch trung tính. Loại phản ứng này gọi là gì?",
    "q.8.2-03.q": "Đọc buret. Số đọc ở đáy mặt khum là bao nhiêu?",
    "q.8.2-04.q": "Lúc đầu buret chỉ 3,5 cm³. Khi chất chỉ thị trong bình chuyển xanh lá, buret chỉ 27,0 cm³. Đã thêm bao nhiêu acid?",
    "q.8.2-05.q": "Một bình kiềm cần đúng 20 cm³ acid để trở thành trung tính. Ali thêm 23 cm³. Dung dịch lúc này thế nào?",
    "q.8.2-05.choices": ["Vẫn pH 7 — đã trung tính thì luôn trung tính", "Hơi acid, khoảng pH 6", "Vẫn kiềm, khoảng pH 13", "Kiềm mạnh vì thêm nhiều chất lỏng hơn"],
    "q.8.2-06.q": "Vì sao kem đánh răng có chứa chất kiềm?",
    "q.8.2-06.choices": ["Để trung hoà acid do vi khuẩn trong miệng tạo ra", "Để kem đánh răng có vị ngọt", "Để diệt hết vi khuẩn bằng cách làm chúng hoá acid", "Để tẩy trắng răng"],
    "q.8.2-07.q": "Một nông dân thấy đất ruộng có pH 5 và cây trồng phát triển kém. Nông dân nên rải gì lên ruộng, và vì sao?",
    "q.8.2-07.choices": ["Vôi, một chất kiềm, để trung hoà acid trong đất", "Giấm, để đất chua hơn", "Muối ăn, để hoà tan acid", "Cát, để nước thoát đi"],
    "q.8.2-08.q": "Để thử pH của đất, em lắc đất với nước rồi lọc hỗn hợp. Dịch lọc (filtrate) là gì?",
    "q.8.2-08.choices": ["Chất lỏng chảy qua giấy lọc", "Phần đất còn lại trên giấy lọc", "Chính tờ giấy lọc", "Chất chỉ thị em nhỏ vào"],
    "q.8.2-09.q": "Đặt một tinh thể washing soda (kiềm mạnh) ở đáy một ống cao chứa nước và chất chỉ thị vạn năng. Rót nhẹ acid lên trên rồi để ống yên vài ngày. Phần nào của ống có tính kiềm mạnh nhất?",
    "q.8.2-09.choices": ["Phần đáy, quanh tinh thể washing soda", "Phần trên cùng, nơi rót acid", "Phần giữa, nơi các màu hoà vào nhau", "Mọi phần đều như nhau"],
    "q.8.2-10.q": "25 cm³ một dung dịch kiềm được trung hoà bởi 20 cm³ một acid. Cần bao nhiêu acid đó để trung hoà 60 cm³ cùng dung dịch kiềm?",
    "q.8.3-01.q": "Câu hỏi nào sau đây có thể kiểm chứng bằng một cuộc khảo sát?",
    "q.8.3-01.choices": ["Thuốc bột nào trung hoà 20 cm³ acid mà dùng ít thìa bột nhất?", "Thuốc chữa khó tiêu nào tốt nhất để mua ở hiệu thuốc?", "Thuốc kháng acid nào có vỏ hộp đẹp nhất và hương vị dễ chịu nhất?", "Thuốc chữa khó tiêu có tốt cho em và cả gia đình không?"],
    "q.8.3-02.q": "Marcus thử ba loại thuốc bột A, B, C để xem mỗi loại cần bao nhiêu thìa để trung hoà cùng một acid. Biến số bạn ấy thay đổi (biến độc lập) là gì?",
    "q.8.3-02.choices": ["Loại thuốc bột", "Số thìa cần dùng", "Thể tích acid", "Màu của chất chỉ thị"],
    "q.8.3-03.q": "Trong cùng cuộc khảo sát, Marcus đo cái gì (biến phụ thuộc)?",
    "q.8.3-03.choices": ["Số thìa bột cần để chất chỉ thị chuyển xanh lá", "Loại thuốc bột được thêm vào mỗi cốc", "Thể tích acid được đổ vào mỗi cốc lúc đầu", "Nhiệt độ phòng trong lúc thêm bột"],
    "q.8.3-04.q": "Chọn TẤT CẢ các biến số Marcus phải giữ nguyên để thí nghiệm công bằng.",
    "q.8.3-04.choices": ["Thể tích acid trong mỗi cốc", "Độ mạnh (pH) của acid", "Số giọt chất chỉ thị", "Cỡ thìa", "Loại thuốc bột", "Số thìa cần dùng"],
    "q.8.3-05.q": "Lan thử mỗi loại bột một lần: A cần 8 thìa, B cần 5, C cần 20. Bạn ấy nói: «Chắc chắn B hiệu quả nhất.» Nhận xét nào hợp lý nhất?",
    "q.8.3-05.choices": ["Lan đúng — một lần thử là đủ chắc chắn", "Lan nên lặp lại mỗi thí nghiệm ít nhất hai lần nữa để kiểm tra kết quả", "Lan nên chọn C vì dùng nhiều bột nhất", "Lan không thể kết luận gì về các loại bột"],
    "q.8.3-06.q": "Bột X cần 14, 17 và 14 thìa trong ba lần thử. Số thìa trung bình là bao nhiêu?",
    "q.8.3-07.q": "Số thìa cần dùng — A: 7, 8, 7 | B: 12, 5, 13 | C: 18, 17, 19. Kết quả nào là bất thường?",
    "q.8.3-07.choices": ["B, lần thử thứ hai (5)", "C, lần thử thứ ba (19)", "A, lần thử thứ hai (8)", "B, lần thử thứ ba (13)"],
    "q.8.3-08.q": "Dùng cùng kết quả (B: 12, 5, 13), tính trung bình của bột B sau khi bỏ kết quả bất thường.",
    "q.8.3-08.howto": "Bỏ đi một kết quả không phù hợp, rồi tính trung bình của các kết quả còn lại.",
    "q.8.3-09.q": "Số thìa trung bình cần để trung hoà cùng một lượng acid — A: 7,3 | B: 12,5 | C: 18. Bột nào hiệu quả nhất?",
    "q.8.3-09.choices": ["A, vì cần ít bột nhất để trung hoà acid", "C, vì số lớn nhất là kết quả tốt nhất", "B, vì nằm ở giữa", "Cả ba hiệu quả như nhau"],
    "q.8.3-10.q": "Minh dùng 20 cm³ cùng một acid cho mỗi cốc, nhưng thêm bột A bằng thìa cà phê còn bột B và C bằng thìa nhỏ. Thí nghiệm có công bằng không?",
    "q.8.3-10.choices": ["Không — lượng bột trong mỗi thìa khác nhau", "Có — thể tích acid như nhau", "Có — bạn ấy vẫn đếm số thìa", "Không — phải dùng acid khác nhau cho mỗi loại bột"],
    "q.8.4-01.q": "Phép thử nào chứng tỏ một chất khí là carbon dioxide?",
    "q.8.4-01.choices": ["Làm nước vôi trong vẩn đục", "Làm que đóm còn tàn đỏ bùng cháy", "Cháy với tiếng nổ «pốp» nhỏ", "Làm chất chỉ thị vạn năng chuyển tím"],
    "q.8.4-02.q": "Cho một miếng táo vào hydrogen peroxide thấy sủi bọt. Que đóm còn tàn đỏ đưa vào ống bùng cháy. Khí sinh ra là khí gì?",
    "q.8.4-02.choices": ["Oxygen", "Hydrogen", "Carbon dioxide", "Hơi nước"],
    "q.8.4-03.q": "Trộn hai dung dịch trong suốt thì xuất hiện chất rắn màu trắng trong chất lỏng. Chất rắn tạo thành như vậy gọi là gì?",
    "q.8.4-04.q": "Chọn TẤT CẢ các quan sát cho thấy có thể đã xảy ra phản ứng hoá học.",
    "q.8.4-04.choices": ["Bột copper oxide đen và acid tạo dung dịch xanh lam", "Có bọt khí khi cho acid vào đá hoa (đá vôi)", "Ống nghiệm nóng lên khi cho kẽm vào acid", "Đường biến mất khi khuấy vào nước", "Nước đá chuyển thành nước lỏng"],
    "q.8.4-05.q": "Một miếng sô-cô-la trong ống nghiệm đặt vào nước nóng. Nó chảy ra và đổi hình dạng. Đã xảy ra phản ứng hoá học chưa?",
    "q.8.4-05.choices": ["Rồi — mọi thay đổi nhìn thấy được đều là phản ứng hoá học", "Chưa — nó chỉ đổi trạng thái, vẫn là sô-cô-la", "Rồi — vì có dùng nhiệt", "Chưa — vì không đổi màu, dù có chất mới tạo thành"],
    "q.8.4-06.q": "Hoàn thành phương trình chữ của phép thử nước vôi trong: calcium hydroxide + carbon dioxide → ______ + water. (Sản phẩm còn thiếu là chất rắn trắng làm nước vôi vẩn đục.)",
    "q.8.4-07.q": "Đun nóng copper oxide màu đen với sulfuric acid loãng thì tạo dung dịch màu xanh lam. Phát biểu nào đúng?",
    "q.8.4-07.choices": ["Sự đổi màu là dấu hiệu đã tạo ra copper sulfate; đun nhẹ, không đun sôi", "Không có chất mới — bột đen chỉ tan ra; đun sôi cho nhanh", "Màu xanh lam là màu của chính acid; đun sôi vẫn an toàn", "Copper oxide là kiềm nên dung dịch phải màu tím"],
    "q.8.4-08.q": "Kết quả — P: muối + nước: tan, nhiệt độ không đổi. Q: bột nở + giấm: sủi bọt. R: sodium hydroxide + acid + chất chỉ thị: tím → xanh lá, bình ấm lên. S: kẽm + acid: sủi bọt, ống nóng lên. Ở thí nghiệm nào KHÔNG xảy ra phản ứng hoá học?",
    "q.8.4-09.q": "Cho dải magnesium vào hydrochloric acid. Nó sủi bọt và dải magnesium «biến mất». Các nguyên tử magnesium đã đi đâu?",
    "q.8.4-09.choices": ["Chúng bị acid phá huỷ nên các nguyên tử magnesium không còn tồn tại", "Chúng kết hợp với chlorine tạo magnesium chloride tan trong dung dịch", "Chúng đều biến thành khí hydrogen mà em thấy sủi lên", "Chúng bay vào không khí dưới dạng khí magnesium không nhìn thấy"],
    "q.8.4-10.q": "Giáo viên thả một mẩu potassium rất nhỏ vào chậu nước lớn sau tấm chắn an toàn. Nó chạy vòng vòng và có ngọn lửa màu tím nhạt. Dấu hiệu nào cho thấy có phản ứng, và vì sao chỉ được làm biểu diễn?",
    "q.8.4-10.choices": ["Có khí (hydrogen) thoát ra và toả nhiều nhiệt đến mức khí bốc cháy; phản ứng rất mạnh nên học sinh không được tự làm", "Chỉ có đổi màu; làm biểu diễn vì potassium đắt", "Potassium nóng chảy nên đây là biến đổi vật lí; tấm chắn để giữ nước sạch", "Không có khí nào; ngọn lửa là do nước cháy"]
  }
});
